 "explain": [
  "hero run 11 (Nov 1 2026), pod hero7c, one GPU, synthetic banks of the released model's shapes rotating over 96 experts (L2 128 MB never serves a repeat), six pairs (one row at top-6), 16 to 24 launches a point under the CUDA profiler.",
  "read peak on this card: 1,535 GB/s (a 1 GiB stream, plain loads or TMA boxes of 32x128 B alike); a 71 MB stream at the gate/up kernel's own program count (864 programs of 80 KB) reads at 1,440 to 1,470 GB/s: ramp and tail cost about 2.5 us a launch at this size.",
  "gate/up bytes a launch 75.2 MB (floor 49.0 us at the peak, 51.5 at the 71 MB stream's rate); down 37.6 MB (24.5 / 25.8).",
  "gate/up with x as a [16, BK] masked tile (the tree before this run): best (32, 256, 2 warps, 2 stages) at 59.9 us; every third stage slower, every wider K tile slower (the x tile was staged per stage, 8 KB at BK 256 against the weight tile's 4 KB, so it set the resident programs an SM).",
  "gate/up with x as one row broadcast in registers and the descriptor made on the host: best (32, 256, 2 warps, 3 stages) at 57.6 us, 1,305 GB/s.",
  "down: best (32, 256, 2 warps, 2 stages) at 33.0 us, 1,142 GB/s (BK 1024 and 512 within 0.1 us, noise); no tile, stage or warp count measured faster, with or without the host descriptor.",
  "measured and not taken: a tile-major bank layout (each TMA box one contiguous 4 KB) 56.4 us (-1.3); two K tiles an iteration 62 to 75 us; gate and up in one program with the SwiGLU, routing weight and per-32 quantization in its epilogue (bit-identical, down then streams h in 31.8 us) but gate/up 70 to 82 us; K tiles of 64 and 128 with 3 to 8 stages 57.9 to 70 us."
